<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yun Bong-gil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1908">1908</a>–<a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yun Bong-gil was a Korean independence activist and member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-gu">Kim Gu</a>'s Korean Patriotic Organisation. On 29 April 1932 he threw a bomb at a Japanese celebration in Hongkew Park, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, killing General Yoshinori Shirakawa and the head of the Japanese residents' association and wounding several officials, including the diplomat Mamoru Shigemitsu. He was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in December 1932.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yun-bong-gil.jpg" alt="Yun Bong-gil" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%B9%E5%A5%89%E5%90%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yun Bong-gil, whose art name was Maeheon, was born in Yesan County, South Chungcheong Province. He left Deoksan Elementary School in 1919 after refusing colonial education and studied Korean and Chinese at a village school. By 1926 he was running evening classes in his home town; he wrote a textbook, Farmers Readers, to teach literacy to poor young adults, founded a group to revive farming villages and set up rural sports clubs. A rural cultural festival he organised brought him to the attention of the Japanese secret police.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, writing that he would not return home alive, and then travelled by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalian">Dalian</a> and Qingdao, where he worked in a factory run by the Korean businessman Park Jin, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in August 1931 and pledging himself to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-gu">Kim Gu</a>. After the fighting in Shanghai in early 1932, Japanese officials planned a victory celebration at Hongkou Park on the emperor's birthday. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-mingshu">Chen Mingshu</a> and Wang Yaqiao, who wanted to disrupt it while Chinese were barred from attending, offered 40,000 yuan through Ahn Chang-ho to the Korean Provisional Government, and Kim Gu chose Yun, who spoke fluent Japanese.</p><p>Yun's bomb, hidden in a water bottle, killed Kawabata Teiji and mortally wounded General Yoshinori Shirakawa, who died on 26 May 1932. Lieutenant General Kenkichi Ueda and Mamoru Shigemitsu each lost a leg and Admiral Kichisaburō Nomura an eye. A second bomb, in a bento box, with which Yun meant to kill himself, failed to explode, and he was arrested. A military court convicted him on 25 May, and he was shot at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> praised him as a young patriot who had done what tens of thousands of Chinese soldiers could not, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee">Syngman Rhee</a> opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-gu">Kim Gu</a>'s strategy of assassination as giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> a pretext for oppression. In 1946 his remains were moved from Nodayama cemetery to Hyochang Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, and in 1962 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> gave him the Order of Merit for National Foundation. Whether his act counts as terrorism is debated: Anders Karlsson and Tessa Morris-Suzuki drew the comparison, while other scholars stress that he attacked only officials.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yun Bong-gil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Kim Gu it organised attacks on Japanese leaders, including <b>Yun Bong-gil's</b> bombing in Shanghai in 1932, then moved through several Chinese cities before settling in Chongqing in 1940, where it formed the Korean Liberation Army and declared war on Japan in 1941.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-republic-of-korea">Provisional Government of the Republic of Korea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yesan County, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.68,126.85&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Yesan County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7735 841 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yun Bong-gil"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8185 1111h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8185 1111h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.68,126.85&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
